.TH RCP 1 "" "" "mcos Manual"
.SH NAME
rcp \- copy files between computers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rcp
.RB [ \-r ]
.I source
.RI [ source ...]
.I target
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rcp
copies files to or from another computer. The sources are all on one side
and the target on the other: a remote name is
.IB host : path
.RI ( host
as
.BR rsh (1)
takes it, found once for the whole copy); a name with no colon, or with a
.B /
before its colon, is local.
.PP
A target that's a directory (or ends with
.BR / )
gets each source by its name in it; otherwise the source becomes the
target. With more than one source, the target must be a directory.
.PP
It's
.B rsh
underneath, as BSD's rcp was: on the other computer,
.B cat
reads or writes each file,
.B mkdir \-p
makes the directories, and
.B find
lists a remote directory's tree.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-r
Copies directories, and everything in them. Without it, a directory
source is refused. A link is copied as what it leads to (a file's
contents); it's never followed into a directory.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when everything was copied; 1 when something couldn't be read or
written (the rest is still copied), or the other computer can't be
reached.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
rcp /notes 1b9c0e:/notes
rcp 1b9c0e:/log /tmp/
rcp /a.lua /b.lua 1b9c0e:/bin/
rcp \-r /projects 1b9c0e:/backup/
.fi
.SH NOTES
BSD's rcp keeps files' times with
.BR \-p ;
this one doesn't. Both ends being remote isn't supported.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rsh (1),
.BR cp (1)
